    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "To survive and reproduce, rodents must be able to efficiently associate environmental cues with the potential presence of a predator or mate. While the neural circuitry for associations, learning, and memory is the subject of a great deal of study, these studies often employ stimuli such as experimenter administered drugs or electric foot shocks.  In order to study more ethologically relevant associations I developed two novel mouse paradigms, and I assessed the importance of the paraventricular thalamic nucleus (PVT) in the formation and retrieval of these associations. The PVT has been implicated in arousal and emotion, with the posterior region of the PVT (pPVT) specifically involved in the learning and memory for appetitive and aversive states. In this study, I explore the role of the pPVT in the contextual learning and memory expression of ethologically relevant mouse behaviors using two novel conditioned place preference paradigms. The first novel paradigm involves conditioning male mice to associate a specific bedding cue with exposure to a female, and the second paradigm involves associating the visual and tactile cues of a traditional conditioned place preference chamber with exposure to a threatening predator odor. I demonstrate the efficacy of these two assays in analyzing desirable social interaction and threat for wildtype mice, and show that silencing the pPVT sessions significantly suppresses memory retrieval and has potentially moderate effects on memory encoding. The data from this study contributes to a body of literature implicating the pPVT’s role in the memory of appetitive and aversive stimuli in laboratory settings, and provides new data on the pPVT’s important role in the ability to recognize and remember environments for ethologically relevant stimuli.",
